HOW A TITLE IS BUILT
A title is a specialization plus a craft noun. The specialization is the words that say what kind of work it is (for example "Backend", "Growth", "Tax", "Machine Learning", "Embedded"). The craft noun is Engineer, Developer, Designer, Accountant, Specialist and the like. Seniority words (Senior, Lead, Principal) are a separate level.
The candidate's own specialization words are the candidate's identity. Words that come from the posting are claims, and the work text has to prove each of them. The target role is the role name of the posting without seniority, team, product or location. Example: the headline "Backend Engineer - Payments Core" gives the target role "Backend Engineer".

DECISION PATH
Go through the steps in order for every title. Stop at the first step that applies.

STEP 1 - ALREADY PRESENT
The specialization of the target role is already in the title, in any order or form. Engineer and Developer count as the same noun. Result: status same, reason starts with "already present".

STEP 2 - SYNONYM
The target specialization is only another name for the field the title already names: the same work, a different fashion of wording. Test: could the former employer's HR swap the two words in the title without asking a single question about what the person did? If they would need to ask anything, it is not a synonym, go to step 3.
Two different technologies, programming languages, tools, industries or professions are never synonyms, even when they are neighbours. A language or tool that the work text does not name is a different thing, not a synonym.
Result: replace the old words with the new ones, status changed, reason starts with "synonym:" followed by both words, for example "synonym: Growth Marketer = Performance Marketer".

STEP 3 - EVIDENCE
The target specialization is a different field, not a synonym. It may enter the title only when the work text shows the candidate doing the core activity of that field as a main part of the job, not as a side detail of other work and not as a component inside something else. Copy the proof phrase from the work text. A phrase that only shows a neighbouring activity is not proof.
With proof, ADD the target specialization to the title and keep the candidate's own specialization words. Never remove the candidate's own specialization to make room. Use the form "Own & Target" or "Target & Own" with the noun shared, for example "Data Analyst & Reporting Developer", or keep the original title and put the target in parentheses.
Result: status changed, reason starts with "evidence:" followed by the proof phrase in single quotes.

STEP 4 - NOTHING
Nothing above applies. Result: status same, reason starts with "none:" and says why in a few words.

HARD LIMITS (apply to every step)
- Never turn the candidate into a different profession. Examples of different professions: analyst and scientist, designer and developer, specialist and manager, individual contributor and lead, engineer and architect, practitioner and consultant. A different noun with the same craft is allowed only through step 2.
- Never add or raise seniority, and never remove a level the candidate has.
- A title may contain the name of a technology, language, tool, industry or domain only if the work text names that same thing.
- Never write a role the candidate never held. If the target is such a role but the work text plainly shows part of its function, you may keep the title and add that function in parentheses, using the plain words of the work text and never the role noun itself. Example: target "Project Manager", work text says "planned the schedule and budget of the migration": "Infrastructure Engineer (migration planning)". If the work text does not show it, leave the title unchanged.
- The golden test for every change: would the candidate's former employer confirm the new wording as an accurate description of the job? If there is any doubt, keep the title.

FORMS
- A title that joins two roles: put the role that matches the target first, then " & ", then the other role exactly as it was.
- kind="main" is the headline under the candidate's name. Keep the name, the separators and the punctuation, and apply the same steps to the role words. If the headline joins specializations with a slash, keep the ones that are not replaced.
- If the title contains the company name or other words around the role, keep them exactly and change only the role words. Never change company names or dates.
- Follow the candidate's note when there is one.

OUTPUT RULES
- Output exactly one tag for every title above, using the same id.
- status="changed" with the new wording as the content, or status="same" with the current wording as the content.
- reason, at most 20 words, must begin with one of: "already present", "synonym:", "evidence:", "none:". For changed it is "synonym:" or "evidence:" only. A changed title with any other reason is not allowed, use same.
- Never use a double quote or the characters < and > inside a reason. Use single quotes for quotations. Plain text only.

OUTPUT FORMAT
<title_suggestion id="0" status="changed" reason="synonym: {old word} = {new word}">{new wording}</title_suggestion>
<title_suggestion id="1" status="changed" reason="evidence: '{phrase from the work text}'">{new wording}</title_suggestion>
<title_suggestion id="2" status="same" reason="already present">{current wording}</title_suggestion>
<title_suggestion id="3" status="same" reason="none: {why}">{current wording}</title_suggestion>